**Decision:** supersede [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) and the
[subject-only triage rule](/kb/triage/dedupe/) for new work. Preserve the accepted record
as history; do not rewrite it. The desk should merge reports only after checking the
same underlying cause. A shared subject is not evidence of a shared cause.

## What #4471 really contains

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) says “Known. One issue, one fix queued.”
Its own executable table disagrees. These are two separate problems, not two names for
one bug. The following table runs the exported classifier against the tenant directory,
after removing overlapping export rows by report ID.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

### Shared parser: month-first interpretation of day-first dates

The shared parser reads month first even when a tenant writes day first. For example,
R-41200 reports 7 April becoming 4 July. The supplied classifier assigns day-first
app/email reports, and day-first booking-v2 form reports, to this group. Fix parsing
against the tenant's date convention; regression coverage must include ambiguous dates
such as 07/04 and a non-ambiguous date such as 23/04. This repo diagnoses the problem;
it does not contain or claim to fix the production parser.

### Booking-v3: a misleading MM/DD/YYYY hint

The booking-v3 form prints MM/DD/YYYY while reading the entered date in a day-first
tenant's convention. That is a separate user-interface contract problem. For a Spanish
booking-v3 tenant, a person following the hint for 6 January may enter 01/06 and get
1 June. R-41230 supplies that symptom. Align the hint with the form's actual locale
interpretation. Test booking-v3 tenants independently of the shared-parser fix.

The two issue counts are mutually exclusive classifier assignments. They are not a
claim that every individual report's root cause has been reproduced. In particular,
the exceptions below stay visible and must be investigated before closing the parser issue.

## Evidence, counts and unresolved exceptions

<EvidenceSummary />

## When reports arrived is not when the desk merged them

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The chart distinguishes receivedAt from mergedAt. Do not interpret the large merge batch
as the start of customer impact. Reports were already arriving in April; the July merge
consolidated an existing queue. Dates in the source retain their stated offsets; weekly
buckets follow the exported component's date-prefix convention, not a new UTC conversion.

## Replacement triage rule

1. Deduplicate export overlap by report ID, not by subject or row count.
2. Read the body, identify the tenant, resolve its locale and booking-form version,
   and record the channel. Browser locale and intakeTag are clues, not ground truth.
3. Use the executable cause logic as a routing aid, then check that the symptom supports it.
   Matching subjects can hide different causes; different subjects can describe the same cause.
4. Merge only with an open ticket for the same evidenced problem. Keep uncertain reports
   in an explicit investigation queue. A missing tenant is not a confirmed duplicate.
5. Preserve conflicting evidence and source IDs. A one-day displacement or an invite
   arriving before booking is not explained merely by a month/day parser label.
6. Recompute report membership and counts when new data arrives. Re-read dependent pages
   and decisions before relying on old prose. Keep received and merged chronology separate.

<DedupeExplorer />

The interactive explorer uses the original exported logic. For example, identical
subjects from alder.physio through form and app resolve to different causes, while
kettle.books app/email reports with different subjects resolve to the same cause.
Unknown tenants need investigation; the classifier does not establish their cause.

## Authority and maintenance

This decision supersedes subject-only merging; it does not mark either product defect fixed.
The old accepted decision remains unchanged for audit. The legacy triage and incident pages
are retained as exported evidence, not instructions to continue using their contradicted prose.
Source material is the support KB export dated 2026-10-04. The review date above records this
analysis, not a claim that the export includes all later desk activity.

Use the [public repository's issues](https://github.com/tmoasz/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues)
for current ticket states. The original JSON, tenant records and classifier are preserved
alongside this page so another reader can reproduce the counts. Every computed table on this
page comes from components; the final desk reference comes from the supplied signature.

